This work reports the initial observation of the $B^0_s\!\to D^{*+}D^{*-}$ decay and measures its branching fraction relative to the $B^0\!\to D^{*+}D^{*-}$ channel. The analysis utilizes a dataset from the LHCb experiment, comprising 9$\,\text{fb}^{-1}$ of proton-proton collision data collected between 2011 and 2018 at center-of-mass energies of 7, 8, and 13$\,\text{TeV}$. The decay is observed with high significance, yielding a branching fraction ratio of \begin{align*}
  \frac{\mathcal{B}(B^0_s\!\to D^{*+}D^{*-})}{\mathcal{B}(B^0\!\to
D^{*+}D^{*-})} = 0.269 \pm 0.032 \pm 0.011 \pm 0.008\, , \end{align*} where the first error is statistical, the second is systematic, and the third arises from the uncertainty in the fragmentation fraction ratio $f_s/f_d$. Consequently, the absolute branching fraction for $B^0_s\!\to D^{*+}D^{*-}$ is calculated as \begin{align*}
  \mathcal{B}(B^0_s\!\to D^{*+}D^{*-}) = (2.15 \pm 0.26 \pm 0.09 \pm 0.06 \pm
0.16)\times 10^{-4} \,, \end{align*} with the final uncertainty stemming from the known branching fraction of $B^0\!\to D^{*+}D^{*-}$.

