This paper investigates normalized ground states for a critical fractional nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) equation with a fixed mass constraint, given by:
\begin{equation*} \begin{cases}
  (-\Delta)^{s}u=\lambda u
+\mu|u|^{q-2}u+|u|^{2_{s}^{\ast}-2}u,&x\in\mathbb{R}^{N},
  \int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}u^{2}dx=a^{2},\\ \end{cases} \end{equation*}
where $(-\Delta)^{s}$ denotes the fractional Laplacian with $0<s<1$ and $N>2s$, $a>0$, $\mu\in \mathbb{R}$, and $2<q<2_{s}^{\ast}=2N/(N-2s)$ represents a fractional critical Sobolev exponent. Leveraging Jeanjean's trick from \cite{Jeanjean} alongside the standard approach described in \cite{Brezis} to address compactness issues, we establish multiple existence and nonexistence theorems regarding the $L^{2}$-subcritical, critical, and supercritical perturbations of the form $\mu|u|^{q-2}u$. Furthermore, we analyze the asymptotic behavior of the derived ground state as $\mu$ approaches $0^{+}$. These findings generalize and enhance previous results across several dimensions.

