This paper provides a comprehensive Hausdorff dimension analysis of real numbers whose continued fraction expansions exhibit a specific growth rate for the product of consecutive partial quotients, while the individual partial quotients grow at a distinct rate. We define the set $\FF(\Phi_1,\Phi_2) \defeq \EE(\Phi_1) \backslash \EE(\Phi_2)$ as:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{x\in[0,1):
\begin{split}
  a_n(x)a_{n+1}(x) & \geq\Phi_1(n) \text{\,\, for infinitely many } n\in\N \\
  a_{n+1}(x) & <\Phi_2(n) \text{\,\, for all sufficiently large } n\in\N
\end{split} \right\},
\end{equation*}
where $\Phi_i:\N\to(0,\infty)$ are functions satisfying $\lim\limits_{n\to\infty} \Phi_i(n)=\infty$. Our findings reveal unexpected outcomes, including conditions under which $\FF(\Phi_1,\Phi_2)$ becomes empty for various non-trivial selections of $\Phi_i$. These results advance the metrical theory of continued fractions by extending several established theorems, notably the primary result of [Nonlinearity, 33(6):2615--2639, 2020]. To derive certain findings, we examine an alternative generalized set of independent interest and compute its Hausdorff dimension. A key component of our approach involves applying the classical mass distribution principle, specifically through the careful allocation of mass on a Cantor subset by introducing a novel concept involving two distinct types of probability measures.

This paper investigates a Neumann problem involving the fractional Laplacian, formulated as:
\begin{equation}\left\{ \begin{array}{rcll} \varepsilon^{2s}(- \Delta)^{s}u + u
&=& f(u) \ \ &\mbox{in} \ \ \Omega \\ \mathcal{N}_{s}u &=& 0 , \,\, &\text{in}
\,\, \mathbb{R}^{N}\backslash \Omega \end{array}\right. \end{equation}
where $\Omega$ is a smooth bounded subset of $\mathbb{R}^{N}$ with $N>2s$, $s$ lies in the interval $(0,1)$, $\varepsilon$ is a positive parameter, and $\mathcal{N}_{s}$ denotes the nonlocal normal derivative defined by Dipierro, Ros-Oton, and Valdinoci. We prove the existence of a small energy solution $u_{\varepsilon}$ that is nonnegative and non-constant. Furthermore, by applying the Moser-Nash iteration technique, we demonstrate that $u_{\varepsilon}$ belongs to $L^{\infty}(\Omega)$.

This study investigates the existence of normalized ground state solutions for a specific biharmonic Choquard-type problem defined as follows:
\begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+(I_\mu*F(u))f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*}
where $\beta\geq0$, $c>0$, $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$, $I_\mu=\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}$ with $\mu\in (0,4)$, $F(u)$ denotes the primitive of $f(u)$, and $f$ is a continuous function exhibiting exponential critical growth consistent with Adams' inequality. By employing a minimax principle grounded in the homotopy stable family, we establish that the problem possesses at least one ground state solution satisfying the normalization constraint.
